  "body": "Maintainer's choice (#452): CamCraft moves from MIT to the Apache License 2.0. The copyright belongs to all CamCraft contributors, not to the maintainer personally.\n\n## What changed\n- `LICENSE` and `aur/LICENSE`: the verbatim Apache-2.0 text from apache.org (SHA-256 `cfc7749b…3d30`).\n- `NOTICE` (new): `Copyright 2026 The CamCraft contributors`. It also states that each contributor holds the rights to their own contributions.\n- `README.md` License section:\n  - the license is Apache-2.0;\n  - the contributors hold the copyright;\n  - pull requests are licensed under Apache-2.0 (§5, inbound = outbound, no CLA);\n  - MIT-era code is redistributed under Apache-2.0, which MIT permits.\n- Packaging:\n  - `aur/PKGBUILD` and `aur/.SRCINFO` say `Apache-2.0`, and the package installs `NOTICE`;\n  - the deb and rpm `License:` fields in `scripts/package-linux.sh` say `Apache-2.0`;\n  - the Linux, FreeBSD and macOS packaging scripts and the Windows zip step in `release.yml` ship `NOTICE` next to `LICENSE`.\n- `docs/decisions/452-license-apache.md`: the decision, the MIT terms the existing code was contributed under, and the options dropped (dual MIT/Apache, a CLA).\n\n## How tested\n- `git grep` finds no MIT reference left for CamCraft's own code. The README mentions MIT only in its history note, and the third-party notices are unchanged.\n- No code changed, so there are no test suites to run.\n\n## Not exercised\n- No packaging run: AUR, deb/rpm, FreeBSD, macOS and the release workflow are only edited. The next release build exercises them.\n\nChangelog: CamCraft is now licensed under Apache-2.0 (was MIT); the copyright belongs to the CamCraft contributors\n\nFixes #452\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
